<% content_for :title, "DBT & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="dbt-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="dbt-bipolar-hero">
    <div class="dbt-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="dbt-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / DBT
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>DBT LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="dbt-bipolar-intro">
        ACCEPT WHAT IS.<br>
        CHANGE WHAT YOU CAN.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / DIALECTICS -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-pink">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        01 / DIALECTICS
      </p>

      <h2>
        TWO THINGS<br>
        CAN BE<br>
        TRUE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        DBT — Dialectical Behavior Therapy — is built around
        something beautifully annoying: reality doesn't always
        fit into either/or.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two things that seem contradictory can both contain truth.
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-dialectic">

        <div>
          <span>THIS IS HARD.</span>
        </div>

        <b>+</b>

        <div>
          <span>I CAN HANDLE THE NEXT PART.</span>
        </div>

        <b>=</b>

        <div>
          <span>BOTH CAN BE TRUE.</span>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        You can have bipolar disorder and still be responsible
        for managing it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your behavior can make sense given what you were experiencing
        and still have hurt somebody.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can accept yourself exactly where you are and still
        want to change.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        I CAN ACCEPT REALITY WITHOUT DECIDING
        IT HAS TO STAY THIS WAY.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / WISE MIND -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-yellow">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        02 / WISE MIND
      </p>

      <h2>
        FEELINGS.<br>
        FACTS.<br>
        BOTH.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        DBT describes emotion mind, reasonable mind,
        and wise mind.
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-minds">

        <div>
          <span>EMOTION MIND</span>
          <p>
            What am I feeling? What does the urge say?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>REASONABLE MIND</span>
          <p>
            What are the facts? What does logic say?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WISE MIND</span>
          <p>
            What do I know when I listen to both?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        The goal isn't to become a robot who never feels anything.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's also not to hand every intense emotion the fucking
        steering wheel.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        FEEL THE FEELING.<br>
        CHECK THE FACTS.<br>
        THEN CHOOSE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / MINDFULNESS -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-black">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        03 / MINDFULNESS
      </p>

      <h2>
        NOTICE<br>
        BEFORE YOU<br>
        REACT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        DBT mindfulness is about noticing what's happening
        without immediately judging it or reacting to it.
      </p>

      <p>
        That can be especially useful when emotions,
        thoughts, or urges arrive at full volume.
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-example">

        <p>THE REACTION:</p>
        <h3>
          "I'M FUCKING LOSING IT."
        </h3>

        <p>THE OBSERVATION:</p>
        <h3>
          "MY HEART IS RACING. I'M ANGRY. I WANT TO REACT."
        </h3>

      </div>

      <p>
        Observation doesn't magically remove the feeling.
      </p>

      <p>
        It gives you information about what's happening
        before the next behavior becomes automatic.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        NOTICE IT.<br>
        NAME IT.<br>
        DON'T IMMEDIATELY BECOME IT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / DISTRESS TOLERANCE -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-purple">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        04 / DISTRESS TOLERANCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        SURVIVE<br>
        THE FUCKING<br>
        MOMENT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        Sometimes the goal isn't fixing everything.
        Sometimes the goal is getting through the next hour
        without making the situation worse.
      </p>

      <p>
        Distress tolerance is for those moments.
      </p>

      <p>
        When you're overwhelmed, furious, panicked, craving,
        agitated, or desperate for immediate relief, the brain
        can start offering very fast solutions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Drink. Use. Text them. Scream. Spend. Quit.
        Blow everything up.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        I DON'T HAVE TO SOLVE MY LIFE RIGHT NOW.
        I JUST HAVE TO NOT MAKE THIS MOMENT WORSE.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Sometimes success is not fixing anything.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes success is simply getting through the wave
        until you're capable of making a better decision.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / EMOTION REGULATION -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-pink">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        05 / EMOTION REGULATION
      </p>

      <h2>
        NAME THE<br>
        DAMN THING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        "I feel terrible" doesn't give you much information.
      </p>

      <p>
        Angry? Ashamed? Scared? Lonely? Rejected?
        Overstimulated? Exhausted? Irritable?
      </p>

      <p>
        Naming an emotion more precisely can help you understand
        what you're actually responding to.
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-regulation">

        <div>
          <span>NAME IT.</span>
          <p>What emotion am I actually experiencing?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CHECK IT.</span>
          <p>Does the intensity fit the facts of the situation?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>NOTICE THE URGE.</span>
          <p>What does this emotion make me want to do?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CHOOSE THE RESPONSE.</span>
          <p>Will following that urge help or hurt?</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        AN EMOTION IS REAL.<br>
        ITS URGE IS NOT A COMMAND.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / OPPOSITE ACTION -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-yellow">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        06 / OPPOSITE ACTION
      </p>

      <h2>
        DON'T FEED<br>
        EVERY<br>
        FEELING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        Sometimes an emotion fits the situation.
        Sometimes its intensity or duration doesn't.
      </p>

      <p>
        When the emotion doesn't fit the facts — or when acting
        on it would make things worse — DBT may use
        <strong>opposite action.</strong>
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-opposite">

        <div>
          <span>THE URGE</span>
          <p>
            Depression says: isolate from everybody.
          </p>
        </div>

        <b>→</b>

        <div>
          <span>OPPOSITE ACTION</span>
          <p>
            Reach out to one safe person.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>THE URGE</span>
          <p>
            Unjustified anger says: attack.
          </p>
        </div>

        <b>→</b>

        <div>
          <span>OPPOSITE ACTION</span>
          <p>
            Step away and respond when the intensity drops.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        This isn't about automatically doing the opposite
        of everything you feel.
      </p>

      <p>
        First check the facts. Then decide whether the emotion
        and its urge actually fit the situation.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / INTERPERSONAL EFFECTIVENESS -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-purple">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        07 / RELATIONSHIPS
      </p>

      <h2>
        SAY WHAT<br>
        YOU FUCKING<br>
        MEAN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        DBT also deals with the complicated business of
        wanting things from other human beings.
      </p>

      <p>
        How do you ask for something?
        Say no?
        Set a boundary?
        Handle conflict?
        Keep your self-respect?
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal isn't winning every argument.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's figuring out what matters in the interaction:
        getting what you need, protecting the relationship,
        protecting your self-respect — or balancing all three.
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-relationship">

        <span>WHAT DO I WANT?</span>
        <span>WHAT DOES THIS RELATIONSHIP NEED?</span>
        <span>WHAT LETS ME RESPECT MYSELF TOMORROW?</span>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        EFFECTIVE DOESN'T ALWAYS MEAN
        PROVING YOU WERE RIGHT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / RADICAL ACCEPTANCE -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-pink">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        08 / RADICAL ACCEPTANCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        REALITY<br>
        DOESN'T NEED<br>
        PERMISSION.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        Radical acceptance means acknowledging reality as it
        actually exists in this moment.
      </p>

      <p>
        It doesn't mean approving of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        It doesn't mean saying something horrible was okay.
      </p>

      <p>
        It doesn't mean refusing to change what can be changed.
      </p>

      <p>
        It means recognizing that reality has already happened.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        I DON'T HAVE TO LIKE THIS.<br>
        BUT THIS IS WHERE I AM STARTING FROM.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        You can hate having bipolar disorder and accept that
        managing bipolar disorder is part of your reality.
      </p>

      <p>
        Acceptance can free up the energy you were spending
        arguing with the fact that something exists.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 / PRACTICE -->
  <section class="dbt-section dbt-black">

    <div class="dbt-section-heading">
      <p class="dbt-number">
        09 / PUT IT INTO PRACTICE
      </p>

      <h2>
        OKAY.<br>
        NOW WHAT?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="dbt-copy">

      <p class="dbt-lead">
        When everything feels like too fucking much,
        you don't need every DBT skill you've ever learned.
        Start with the next useful one.
      </p>

      <div class="dbt-practice-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>
            <strong>STOP.</strong><br>
            Don't automatically act on the first urge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>
            <strong>OBSERVE.</strong><br>
            What am I thinking, feeling, and wanting to do?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>
            <strong>CHECK THE FACTS.</strong><br>
            Does the emotion fit what's actually happening?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>
            <strong>GET THROUGH THE PEAK.</strong><br>
            If you're overwhelmed, focus on not making
            the situation worse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>
            <strong>FIND WISE MIND.</strong><br>
            What do emotion and reason each have to tell me?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>06</span>
          <p>
            <strong>CHOOSE EFFECTIVENESS.</strong><br>
            What response actually moves me toward what matters?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>07</span>
          <p>
            <strong>HOLD BOTH.</strong><br>
            I can accept myself right now and still work
            toward something different.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="dbt-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="dbt-ending-small">
        YOU CAN ACCEPT THIS MOMENT.
      </p>

      <h2>
        AND STILL<br>
        <span>CHANGE IT.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="dbt-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Feelings can be valid without every action being effective.
      </p>

      <p>
        Acceptance and change can coexist.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can be doing your best and need to do better.
      </p>

      <p>
        DBT lives in that messy space between opposites —
        where reality usually lives too.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "dbt-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>